{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And if it is on the bed.</b> One may say: and if it is on the bed — <b>הוא</b> refers back to 'a person who has a discharge.' And so the interpretation will be: if that blood is on her bed or on the vessel of her seat, <b>when one touches it</b> — that blood on that vessel — <b>he becomes impure until evening</b>, and after immersion. A parallel: ״and every garment on which there is a seed of emission.״ And if you ask: why is ״and if it is on the bed״ stated? If one who touches her bed is impure, one who touches her blood — all the more so! But because we do not punish from an inference of <i>kal va-chomer</i>, it wrote for you <b>and if it is on the bed</b>. And so we derive similarly regarding ״all who come into the tent״ and ״all that is in the tent shall be impure.״"], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:12:32.764738710Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}